TP.register({id:"lp5-g5",lp:5,grade:"5",mode:"curriculum",checks:4,nextGrade:"6th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const FLU_TITLE="Morning at the Weather Station";const FLUENCY="Dawn at base camp came as a thin gray line along the frozen sea. Base camp is the cluster of tents where the team sleeps and keeps its food. Tomas pulled on two pairs of mittens before he unzipped the tent, because at thirty degrees below zero bare skin stings in seconds. His job that morning was the weather station, a small white box on legs at the edge of camp. Inside the box, a thermometer and a wind gauge had been recording all night. He copied the numbers into the logbook with a pencil, since ink freezes. The temperature had dropped four degrees since midnight. Out on the ice, a seal lifted its head beside a breathing hole, watched him for a moment, and slid under without a sound. Tomas closed the box, pushed the logbook inside his coat, and turned back toward the cook tent, where the radio would soon crackle with the morning call from the ship.";const SLED_TITLE="The Spare Sled";const SLED=["Nadia had been the youngest person at the research station for three weeks, and she had not once been allowed to drive a dogsled. Her aunt, who ran the dog team, said the trail to the depot was no place to learn. A depot is a small shed where a team leaves food and fuel along a route, so the sleds do not have to carry everything at once.","On Thursday the radio brought word that a storm would reach camp by evening. Two sleds had to go out to the depot and back before the wind rose. Nadia counted the drivers at the breakfast table. There were only two, and one of them was her aunt.","Her aunt set down her mug. “Harness the spare team,” she said. “Stay in my tracks. If you cannot see my sled, stop and wait for me.”","Nadia was out the door before the words were finished. She checked every buckle twice, the way she had watched her aunt do it, and she did not say one word about being cold the whole way out."].join("\n\n");const LIGHTS_TITLE="Lights Over the Ridge";const LIGHTS="The cook banged the pot lid twice, which was the camp signal for come outside now. Yusuf pushed out of the tent still holding his spoon. Above the ridge, the northern lights were awake. The northern lights are bands of green and pink light that appear on some clear nights near the top of the world. Tonight they hung over the camp like a green curtain in an open window, folding and lifting with no wind to move them. One long band was a river of light pouring north, and the snow under it turned a faint green, as if the ground had decided to glow too. Nobody spoke. The cold pinched Yusuf’s cheeks, but he was not going inside. The dogs sat up in a row, quiet as stones. When the last band faded, the sky was an empty stage, and the camp slowly remembered how to talk.";const ICE_TITLE="Reading the Ice";const ICE=["A research team cannot tell how thick sea ice is just by looking at it. Fresh ice and year-old ice look much the same under a layer of snow. So before a sled crosses, someone drills a hole and drops in a measuring tape with a small metal bar on the end. The bar catches on the bottom edge of the ice, and the tape shows the thickness.","Teams follow a simple rule: a loaded sled needs at least twenty centimeters of solid ice under it. Dark patches are a warning, because dark ice is often thin ice with seawater showing through.","The drill tells the team one more thing. Each winter adds a new layer to the ice from below, so a core pulled out of thick ice has bands in it, a little like the rings in a tree trunk. Counting the bands tells the scientists how many winters that piece of ice has lasted."].join("\n\n");const SOURCES=[["Source 1","<i>Cold Water, Big Meals</i>, a book by Elena Marsh","In summer, the sun shines on polar water almost all day. Tiny floating plants called plankton grow fast in that light. Small animals called krill eat the plankton, and krill gather in swarms so thick that the water looks cloudy. A hungry whale can take in thousands of krill in one mouthful."],["Source 2","“Tracking the Summer Visitors,” a magazine article by Dawit Bekele","Scientists stick small tags to whales and follow them by satellite. The tags show that some whales swim more than four thousand miles to reach polar water in summer. They feed for a few months, build a thick layer of fat, and then swim back to warmer water, where their calves are born. Polar water in winter is far too icy for a newborn calf."]];const WORDS=["expedition","reflection","irregular","mismanaged","incredible","submarine","geologist","nonrenewable","transportable","unreliable","plurmation","unslickable"];const CAMP_FACTS=["Push the tent stakes in, then pile snow on them so they hold.","Melt clean snow on the stove to get drinking water.","Stamp the snow flat with skis or boots to make a pad for the tent.","Park the sled beside the tent so blowing snow does not bury it.","Pick a spot behind a ridge, where the ridge blocks the wind.","Set the tent door on the side away from the wind."];const CAMP_BOX=["Base camp is a cluster of tents where the team sleeps and stores food.","Supplies ride on sleds pulled by dogs or by people on skis.","Food is carried as dried rations, so the sleds stay light.","Drinking water comes from melting snow on a stove.","The temperature can fall to forty degrees below zero, cold enough to freeze the ink in a pen.","A depot is a shed or a buried box of food and fuel left along the route."];const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:8px 18px;margin:14px auto 0;max-width:840px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 4px">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:22px;font-size:1.2rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const factCards=items=>`<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:12px;margin-top:16px">${items.map(x=>`<div style="border:3px solid var(--line);border-radius:14px;padding:10px 14px;background:var(--surface);font-size:1.2rem;line-height:1.35">${x}</div>`).join("")}</div>`;const sourcesHtml=()=>`<div class="texts">${SOURCES.map(t=>`<article><h3>${t[0]}</h3><p class="small" style="margin:0 0 6px">${t[1]}</p><p>${t[2]}</p></article>`).join("")}</div>`;const pills=items=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:18px;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">${items.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.2rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;function mapSquare(){const ox=120,oy=36,S=240,cw=S/3,rh=S/2;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 460 330" width="440" role="img" aria-label="A square map section cut into 2 rows and 3 columns. A part 2 columns long and 1 row wide is shaded.">`;for(let r=0;r<2;r++)for(let c=0;c<3;c++)s+=`<rect x="${ox+c*cw}" y="${oy+r*rh}" width="${cw}" height="${rh}" fill="${r<1&&c<2?"var(--sea-soft)":"var(--surface)"}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`;const t=(x,y,txt,a)=>`<text x="${x}" y="${y}" text-anchor="${a||"middle"}" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">${txt}</text>`;s+=`<line x1="${ox}" y1="${oy-10}" x2="${ox+2*cw}" y2="${oy-10}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`+t(ox+cw,oy-18,"2/3 mile");s+=`<line x1="${ox-10}" y1="${oy}" x2="${ox-10}" y2="${oy+rh}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`+t(ox-18,oy+rh/2+6,"1/2 mile","end");s+=t(ox+S/2,oy+S+28,"1 mile")+t(ox+S+14,oy+S/2+6,"1 mile","start");return s+"</svg>"}const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const BREAKER="The ship that brings the team in is an icebreaker. Its bow is not shaped like a knife. It slopes, so that when the ship drives forward the hull rides up onto the ice instead of pushing against it, and the weight of the ship breaks the sheet underneath. The hull is wider at the waterline than it is lower down, so broken slabs slide out to the sides rather than scraping along the plates. An icebreaker moves slowly, sometimes backing up and charging the same spot again, and it burns a great deal of fuel. Behind it the broken channel stays open for a few hours, long enough for another ship to follow.";const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Radio Check",short:"Radio Read",parts:[{std:["RF.5.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:95,dir:"Read the story out loud at your normal speed. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level passage read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(FLUENCY,FLU_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute, mark the last word read, and count errors on your own copy. Let the student finish the passage.","Grade 5 winter benchmark (Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017): 50th percentile 133 words correct per minute, 25th percentile 109, 10th percentile 84.","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for a pause at the commas in the long sentence about the seal, and for self-corrections that show the student is reading for meaning."]}]},{id:2,title:"Sled Load Fractions",short:"Sled Loads",parts:[{std:["5.NF.A.1"],dur:140,dir:"Solve on your paper. Show your work and hold it up.",desc:"adding and subtracting fractions and mixed numbers with unlike denominators",html:()=>`<p class="big">Every sled on the expedition is packed in parts of a full load. Work these out.</p>`,qs:["1/2 + 2/5 = ?","3/4 − 2/7 = ?","2/3 + 5/6 = ?","4 1/4 + 2 2/3 = ?","5 1/6 − 2 3/4 = ?"],key:["9/10. Rename with 10 as the denominator: 5/10 + 4/10. Or: twentieths work too, 10/20 + 8/20 = 18/20. Partial: 3/7 comes from adding across and does not count. (5.NF.A.1)","13/28. Rename with 28: 21/28 − 8/28. Partial: subtracting across, numerator from numerator and denominator from denominator, does not count. (5.NF.A.1)","9/6, which is the same as 3/2 or 1 1/2. Rename with 6: 4/6 + 5/6. Partial: 9/6 right with no simpler form given is approaching. (5.NF.A.1)","6 11/12. Add the wholes to get 6, then 3/12 + 8/12 = 11/12. Or: change both to improper fractions, 51/12 + 32/12 = 83/12. Partial: 6 3/7 comes from adding across and does not count. (5.NF.A.1)","2 5/12. Rename with 12: 5 2/12 − 2 9/12. Regroup one whole to get 4 14/12 − 2 9/12. Or: change both to improper fractions, 62/12 − 33/12 = 29/12. Partial: the renaming right with no regrouping is approaching. (5.NF.A.1)"]}]},{id:3,title:"The Spare Sled",short:"Spare Sled",parts:[{std:["RL.5.1"],dur:110,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words from the story that prove each answer.",desc:"quoting a story accurately to tell what it says and to back up inferences",html:()=>F.passage(SLED,SLED_TITLE,true),qs:["What is a depot? Read me the words from the story that tell you.","How did Nadia feel when her aunt said to harness the spare team? The story never names the feeling. Read me the sentence that proves your answer.","Why did her aunt let her drive that day, after saying no for three weeks? Read me the words that back up your answer.","Did Nadia want to show she could be trusted with the team? Read me words that show it."],key:["A small shed where a team leaves food and fuel along a route. Quotes: “A depot is a small shed where a team leaves food and fuel along a route, so the sleds do not have to carry everything at once.” The student should read the words as written, not retell them loosely. (RL.5.1, explicit)","Excited, eager, or thrilled. Quotes: “Nadia was out the door before the words were finished.” (RL.5.1, inference)","A storm was coming and there were not enough drivers. Accept either quote: “Two sleds had to go out to the depot and back before the wind rose,” or “There were only two, and one of them was her aunt.” (RL.5.1, inference)","Yes. Quotes: “She checked every buckle twice, the way she had watched her aunt do it, and she did not say one word about being cold the whole way out.” (RL.5.1, inference)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Ration Math",short:"Rations",parts:[{std:["5.NF.A.2"],dur:160,dir:"Solve the first four on your paper. Explain number 5 out loud.",desc:"fraction word problems about supplies and distance, plus a benchmark fraction check",html:()=>`<p class="big">Rations are the packs of dried food a team eats each day. Solve these supply problems.</p>`,qs:["On Monday the team skied 3/4 of a mile out to the flag line and 7/8 of a mile back by a longer route. How far did they ski in all?","A fuel can was 5/6 full at breakfast. The stove used 1/4 of a can. How much of the can is left?","One crate holds 2 1/2 days of rations. A second crate holds 1 3/4 days of rations. How many days of rations is that together?","A sled started out with 3 1/3 kilograms of dog food. The team used 1 1/2 kilograms. How much is left?","A radio message says the team walked 2/5 of a mile in the morning. It says they walked 1/3 of a mile in the afternoon. It gives a total of 3/8 of a mile. Without adding, how can you tell the total is wrong?"],key:["13/8 miles, which is 1 5/8 miles. 3/4 + 7/8 = 6/8 + 7/8. Partial: 13/8 right with no mixed number given is approaching. (5.NF.A.2)","7/12 of the can. 5/6 − 1/4 = 10/12 − 3/12. Partial: 4/2 comes from subtracting across and does not count. (5.NF.A.2)","4 1/4 days. 2 1/2 + 1 3/4 = 2 2/4 + 1 3/4 = 3 5/4. Or: change both to quarters, 10/4 + 7/4 = 17/4. Partial: 3 5/4 left unrenamed is approaching. (5.NF.A.2)","1 5/6 kilograms. 3 1/3 − 1 1/2 = 10/3 − 3/2 = 20/6 − 9/6 = 11/6. Or: take 1 off 3 1/3 to get 2 1/3, then take the other 1/2 to get 1 5/6. Partial: 11/6 right with no mixed number given is approaching. (5.NF.A.2)","A total has to be bigger than each part, and 3/8 is smaller than 2/5 on its own. Benchmark reasoning: 2/5 and 1/3 are each close to 1/2, so the total should be close to 1, but 3/8 is less than 1/2. Partial: saying the total is wrong with no benchmark used is approaching. (5.NF.A.2, benchmark fractions and reasonableness)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Crate Label Words",short:"Crate Words",parts:[{std:["RF.5.3"],kind:"words",words:WORDS,n:12,dur:70,dir:"Read each word out loud. The last two are made-up words. Just sound them out.",desc:"reading long words using prefixes, suffixes, roots, and syllable chunks",key:["Read each word. plurmation and unslickable are made-up words, so the student has to use chunks and word parts instead of memory: plur-ma-tion, un-slick-a-ble.","Watch for prefixes (ir-, mis-, in-, sub-, non-, trans-, un-, re-), suffixes (-tion, -ible, -able, -ist, -ed), base words (regular, manage, rely, new, flect for bend), and roots (marine for sea, geo for earth, port for carry).","Watch whether the student breaks a long word into syllable chunks (ex-pe-di-tion, ge-ol-o-gist, non-re-new-a-ble, trans-port-a-ble, un-re-li-a-ble) or guesses from the first few letters."]}]},{id:6,title:"Reading the Ice",short:"Read the Ice",parts:[{std:["RI.5.1"],dur:100,dir:"Read the article. Then answer out loud with the exact words from the article.",desc:"quoting an informational text accurately to tell what it says and to back up inferences",html:()=>F.passage(ICE,ICE_TITLE,true),qs:["Why can a team not tell how thick the sea ice is just by looking? Read me the exact words that answer this.","Read me the sentence that tells how thick the ice has to be before a loaded sled crosses it.","A driver sees a wide dark patch on the ice ahead. Should the sled cross it? Read me the words that back up your answer.","Which piece of ice has lasted more winters, one with three bands in its core or one with seven? Read me the words that prove it."],key:["Quotes: “Fresh ice and year-old ice look much the same under a layer of snow.” (RI.5.1, explicit)","Quotes: “Teams follow a simple rule: a loaded sled needs at least twenty centimeters of solid ice under it.” (RI.5.1, explicit)","No. Quotes: “Dark patches are a warning, because dark ice is often thin ice with seawater showing through.” (RI.5.1, inference)","The one with seven bands. Quotes: “Each winter adds a new layer to the ice from below,” or “Counting the bands tells the scientists how many winters that piece of ice has lasted.” (RI.5.1, inference)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Share the Fuel",short:"Fuel Shares",parts:[{std:["5.NF.B.3"],dur:115,dir:"Solve on your paper. Then tell your teacher your answers.",desc:"fractions as division, with sharing problems that have fraction and mixed number answers",html:()=>`<p class="big">Everything at base camp gets split into equal shares. Solve these.</p>`,qs:["7 bags of dried fruit are shared equally by 8 people in the cook tent. How much of a bag does each person get?","Write 23 ÷ 6 as a fraction and as a mixed number.","A 17 kilogram box of dog food is split equally among 5 dog teams. How many kilograms does each team get? Between which two whole numbers is your answer?","4 liters of stove fuel are poured equally into 3 bottles. How much fuel goes in each bottle? Tell how you know the answer is more than 1 liter."],key:["7/8 of a bag. 7 ÷ 8 = 7/8. Partial: 8/7 reverses the share and does not count. (5.NF.B.3)","23/6 and 3 5/6. Partial: one of the two forms given is approaching. (5.NF.B.3)","17/5 kilograms, which is 3 2/5 kilograms. It is between 3 and 4. Partial: 17/5 right with no pair of whole numbers named is approaching. (5.NF.B.3)","4/3 of a liter, which is 1 1/3 liters. The first 3 liters give each bottle 1 whole liter, and the last liter still has to be split 3 ways. Partial: 4/3 right with no reason that it is more than 1 is approaching. (5.NF.B.3)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Whale Watch Notes",short:"Whale Notes",parts:[{std:["W.5.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:215,dir:"Read both sources. Write your notes on your paper and hold them up.",desc:"notes in the student’s own words from two sources, a list of sources, and a spoken summary",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem">Question: Why do some whales travel to polar water in summer?</p>${sourcesHtml()}<ol class="qlist"><li>Write 3 short notes that answer the question. Use your own words. A note is a few words, not a whole sentence. Use both sources.</li><li>Under your notes, list your sources. Write the title and the author of each one.</li><li>Now look only at your notes. Answer the question out loud in one or two sentences.</li></ol>`,key:["Writes 3 notes that answer the question and uses both sources, such as: all-day summer sun, plankton grow fast, thick krill swarms, thousands of krill in one mouthful, swim over 4,000 miles, feed a few months, build fat, go back to warm water for calves. (W.5.8, gathers relevant information)","Notes are short and in the student’s own words, not sentences copied from the sources. (W.5.8, paraphrases in notes)","Lists both sources by title and author: Cold Water, Big Meals by Elena Marsh, and “Tracking the Summer Visitors” by Dawit Bekele. (W.5.8, provides a list of sources)","Sums up the answer out loud from the notes, in the student’s own words, with correct facts: polar water in summer is full of food, so whales go there to eat and build fat. (W.5.8, summarizes in finished work)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Lights Over the Ridge",short:"Sky Lights",parts:[{std:["RL.5.4"],dur:95,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud.",desc:"explaining similes, metaphors, and words as they are used in a story",html:()=>F.passage(LIGHTS,LIGHTS_TITLE,true),qs:["“Tonight they hung over the camp like a green curtain in an open window.” Is that a simile or a metaphor? How do you know? What does it tell you about the lights?","“One long band was a river of light pouring north.” What is being compared to a river? What does the author mean?","Find the simile that tells how the dogs sat. What does it mean?","“The sky was an empty stage.” Simile or metaphor? What does it tell you about the sky after the lights faded?","What does the word pinched mean in this story? Which words helped you know?"],key:["A simile, because it uses the word like. It tells you the light hung in soft folds and lifted and moved slowly, the way a curtain does. (RL.5.4, simile)","The long band of light. A river is long and keeps moving in one direction, so the band of light looked like it was flowing north across the sky. (RL.5.4, metaphor)","“Quiet as stones.” The dogs sat completely still and made no sound. (RL.5.4, simile)","A metaphor, because it says the sky was a stage with no like or as. The sky was bare and still, and the show was over. (RL.5.4, metaphor)","Stung or nipped his skin, the way a pinch does. Clues: the cold is doing it, and “he was not going inside,” so it hurt a little but not enough to send him in. (RL.5.4, meaning of a word as used in the text)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Map Square",parts:[{std:["5.NF.B.4"],dur:115,dir:"Solve on your paper. For number 4, use the picture. Then tell your teacher your answers.",desc:"multiplying a whole number by a fraction and a fraction by a fraction, with an area model",qs:["2/5 × 7 = ?","3/8 × 4/9 = ?","The team packed 24 route flags. 5/6 of them were planted along the trail. How many flags were planted?","Look at the picture. The big square is one map square, 1 mile by 1 mile. The shaded patch of thin ice is 2/3 mile long and 1/2 mile wide. Write a multiplication equation for the area of the patch."],fig2:()=>`<div class="figure">${mapSquare()}</div>`,key:["14/5, which is 2 4/5. Write 7 as 7/1 and multiply across. Or: 1/5 of 7 is 7/5, and two of those is 14/5. (5.NF.B.4)","12/72, which is the same as 1/6. Or: cancel the 3 into the 9 and the 4 into the 8 first, which leaves 1/6. (5.NF.B.4)","20 flags. 24 split into 6 equal groups is 4, and 5 groups of 4 is 20. Or: 5/6 × 24 = 120/6 = 20. (5.NF.B.4)","2/3 × 1/2 = 2/6, which is the same as 1/3 of a square mile. The square is cut into 6 equal parts and 2 of them are shaded. Partial: the equation right with no area given is approaching. (5.NF.B.4)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Logbook Fix-Ups",short:"Logbook Fix",parts:[{std:["L.5.1"],dur:70,dir:"Each logbook line needs a fix. Say the whole sentence out loud the right way.",desc:"spoken grammar items (verb tense shifts, perfect tenses, paired conjunctions)",qs:["Yesterday the team packed the crates, loads the sled, and left at dawn.","Since Tuesday, the wind has blow straight out of the north.","By the time we reached the depot, the storm already covered our tracks.","Not only was the radio wet, and the battery was dead.","Say this with the right form of the verb travel: “By Friday, the team ____ ninety miles.”"],key:["Yesterday the team packed the crates, loaded the sled, and left at dawn. (L.5.1, fixing a shift in verb tense)","Since Tuesday, the wind has blown straight out of the north. (L.5.1, present perfect tense)","By the time we reached the depot, the storm had already covered our tracks. (L.5.1, past perfect tense)","Not only was the radio wet, but the battery was also dead. (L.5.1, correlative conjunctions not only/but also)","By Friday, the team will have traveled ninety miles. (L.5.1, future perfect tense)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Radio Report",short:"Report",parts:[{std:["SL.5.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:95,dir:"Plan for 1 minute. Then give your report out loud, like a radio call to the ship.",desc:"reporting on a topic out loud with facts in a logical order and clear speech",html:()=>`<p class="big">Give a report, about 1 minute long, that tells the ship how your team sets up camp for the night. The cards are mixed up. Tell the steps in an order that makes sense, and add details of your own.</p>${factCards(CAMP_FACTS)}`,key:["Starts by telling what the report is about (SL.5.4, reports on a topic)","Puts the steps in a sensible order: pick a spot behind a ridge, stamp a flat pad, set the tent with the door away from the wind, stake it and pile snow on the stakes, park the sled beside the tent, melt snow for water (SL.5.4, sequences ideas logically)","Uses at least four of the cards and adds a fact or descriptive detail of the student’s own (SL.5.4, appropriate facts and relevant details)","Ends with a closing sentence (SL.5.4)","Speaks clearly at a pace that is easy to follow (SL.5.4)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Field Guide Page",short:"Write",parts:[{std:["W.5.2"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:300,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write a field guide page that teaches a reader who has never been anywhere cold how a polar research team lives at base camp. Use at least three facts from the fact box, in your own words. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${factBox("Fact box: life at base camp",CAMP_BOX)}${checklist(["Start by naming your topic","Put facts that go together next to each other","Use linking words: also, another, for example, because","Use the topic words correctly: base camp, sled, rations, depot","End with a closing sentence"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly (a title or heading counts as formatting) (W.5.2)","Groups related information in an order that makes sense (W.5.2)","Develops the topic with at least three facts or details from the fact box, written in the student’s own sentences (W.5.2)","Links ideas with words and phrases such as also, another, for example, because, in contrast (W.5.2)","Ends with a concluding sentence (W.5.2)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Two Whale Sources",short:"Two Sources",parts:[{std:["RI.5.9"],n:2,dur:60,dir:"Read both sources. Then answer out loud, using both of them in each answer.",desc:"pulling information from two texts on one topic together to speak about it knowledgeably",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Both sources below are about whales that feed in polar water.</p>${sourcesHtml()}`,qs:["Use both sources together. Explain why whales swim so far to polar water in summer and then swim back.","A friend asks what the whales find when they get there and how anyone knows where they go. Answer in one connected explanation, using both sources."],key:["From Source 1: long summer sunlight grows plankton fast, krill eat the plankton and gather in swarms so thick the water looks cloudy, and a whale can take thousands of krill in one mouthful. From Source 2: the whales feed for a few months, build a thick layer of fat, and then swim back to warmer water where their calves are born, because polar water in winter is far too icy for a newborn calf. Credit the answer only when it uses at least one fact from each source. (RI.5.9)","The food answer comes from Source 1 (plankton, then krill swarms, then whales feeding) and the knowing answer comes from Source 2 (scientists stick small tags to whales and follow them by satellite, which showed trips of more than four thousand miles). Credit the answer when the two sources are joined into one explanation rather than listed one after the other. (RI.5.9)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Breaking the Ice",short:"Listen",parts:[{std:["SL.5.2"],n:3,dur:60,read:BREAKER,dir:"Put your pencil down and listen. Then answer out loud.",desc:"summarizing a text read aloud",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="text-align:center;margin-top:0">Listening time. Your teacher will read a short report about the ship that brings the team in.</p><p class="small" style="text-align:center">The bow is the front of a ship. The hull is its body. The waterline is where the water touches it.</p>`,key:["Ask: Summarize what I read, in two or three sentences. (An icebreaker has a sloping bow so it rides up onto the ice and lets its own weight break the sheet, and a hull that is widest at the waterline so the broken pieces slide aside. It is slow and burns a lot of fuel, and it leaves an open channel behind it for a few hours. A summary covers the whole report in the student's own words, not just one fact and not the whole thing repeated back.) (SL.5.2)","Ask: Summarize just the part about why the bow slopes. (A sloping bow lets the ship climb onto the ice, so the ship's weight does the breaking instead of the ship pushing against the ice edge.) (SL.5.2)","Ask: Name one detail you left out of your summary, and say why you left it out. (Any detail the student skipped, such as backing up and charging the same spot, with a reason like it is an example rather than a main point.) (SL.5.2)","Read the report once at a steady pace. If you read it a second time, note that you did."]}]},{id:16,title:"Dogs or Skis",short:"Opinion",parts:[{std:["W.5.1"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:75,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion piece with an ordered set of reasons supported by facts",html:()=>`<p class="big">A small team has to reach a depot and come back the same day. Should they go with a dog team, or on skis pulling their own light sleds? Write your opinion. Write 4 or 5 sentences.</p>${factBox("Facts you may use",["A dog team pulls a heavy load and keeps a steady pace all day.","Dogs have to be fed, harnessed, and cared for every single day.","Skis need no food and nothing to repair, and a skier can turn back alone.","A skier can only pull a light sled, so less can be carried.","A dog team often stops at thin ice on its own.","A depot is a shed or buried box of food and fuel left along the route."])}${checklist(["State your opinion in the first sentence","Give two reasons, each backed by a fact or detail","Put the stronger reason where it does the most good","Use linking words: because, for instance, in addition, as a result","End with a closing sentence"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic and states a clear opinion (W.5.1)","Gives reasons that are ordered logically, not just listed in any order (W.5.1)","Supports the reasons with facts and details, such as the load a dog team pulls, the daily care dogs need, or that skis need no food (W.5.1)","Links the opinion and the reasons with words or phrases such as because, for instance, in addition, as a result, and ends with a concluding statement (W.5.1)"]}]},{id:17,title:"Out to the Depot",short:"Narrative",parts:[{std:["W.5.3"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:75,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative with an orientation, ordered events, dialogue or description, and a conclusion",html:()=>`<p class="big">The story of the spare sled stops just as Nadia heads out. Write what happens next on her first drive to the depot and back. Write 5 or 6 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Set it up: who is there, where they are, what is happening","Put the events in an order the reader can follow","Use something someone says, or a description, to bring one moment to life","Use words that move the reader through time: at first, by then, once, finally","Finish the story"])}${F.passage(SLED,SLED_TITLE,true)}`,key:["Orients the reader: the situation, who is telling it, and who is in it (W.5.3)","Events unfold naturally in an order the reader can follow (W.5.3)","Uses dialogue, description, or pacing to develop at least one event or one character (W.5.3)","Uses transitional words or phrases to show the sequence, and ends with a conclusion that follows from the events (W.5.3)","The new writing has to fit the story on the screen: Nadia drives the spare team, stays in her aunt's tracks, and a storm is due by evening. Note any part that contradicts it."]}]},{id:18,title:"Brackets on the Load Sheet",short:"Grouping",parts:[{std:["5.OA.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Say each answer out loud.",desc:"evaluating expressions with parentheses and brackets, and writing one for a situation",qs:["Evaluate 6 × (14 + 9).","Evaluate 2 × [18 + (5 × 4)].","The team takes 3 sleds. Each sled carries 12 ration boxes and 8 fuel cans. Write one expression with parentheses for the total number of items on all three sleds, then evaluate it."],key:["138. The parentheses go first: 14 + 9 = 23, then 6 × 23 = 138. Partial: 93 comes from multiplying before adding and does not count. (5.OA.A.1)","76. Innermost first: 5 × 4 = 20, then 18 + 20 = 38, then 2 × 38 = 76. Partial: 38 found by stopping inside the brackets is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)","3 × (12 + 8) = 60 items. The parentheses are needed, because 3 × 12 + 8 would be 44 and that counts only the ration boxes three times. Partial: 60 found with no expression written is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Ten Times and a Tenth",short:"Place Value",parts:[{std:["5.NBT.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud. Explain how you know.",desc:"a digit worth ten times the digit to its right and one tenth of the digit to its left",html:()=>`<p class="huge" style="margin:4px 0">3,330</p><p class="small" style="text-align:center;margin-top:0">meters of sled track cut this week</p>`,qs:["In 3,330, how does the 3 in the hundreds place compare with the 3 in the tens place?","In the gauge reading 0.55, how does the 5 in the hundredths place compare with the 5 in the tenths place?","What is one tenth of 400, and in which place does that digit sit?"],key:["It is worth 10 times as much. The hundreds 3 is worth 300 and the tens 3 is worth 30. Partial: naming the two values with no comparison is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)","It is worth one tenth as much. The tenths 5 is 5/10 and the hundredths 5 is 5/100, and 5/100 is one tenth of 5/10. Partial: 10 times as much reverses the comparison and does not count. (5.NBT.A.1)","40, and the 4 sits in the tens place. Each step to the right is one tenth of the place before it. Partial: 40 right with no place named is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)"]}]},{id:20,title:"Ice Gauge Decimals",short:"Decimals",parts:[{std:["5.NBT.A.3"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Answer out loud. Write numbers 1 and 2 on your paper and hold them up.",desc:"reading, writing, and comparing decimals to thousandths",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The ice gauge gives a thickness in meters, to three decimal places.</p>`,qs:["The gauge reads 0.246 meters. Read that number out loud, then write it in expanded form.","Write three and forty-seven hundredths as a decimal.","Compare 0.246 and 0.25 with the > or < sign. Explain how you decided."],key:["Two hundred forty-six thousandths. Expanded form: 0.2 + 0.04 + 0.006, or 2 × (1/10) + 4 × (1/100) + 6 × (1/1000). Saying zero point two four six is a start, but the standard asks for the number name. Partial: the number name right with no expanded form, or the reverse, is approaching. (5.NBT.A.3)","3.47. Partial: 3.047 reads hundredths as thousandths and does not count. (5.NBT.A.3)","0.246 < 0.25. Writing 0.25 as 0.250 puts them in the same unit: 246 thousandths against 250 thousandths. Comparing digit by digit from the left also works: the tenths match at 2, and then 4 hundredths is less than 5 hundredths. Partial: the right choice with the sign the wrong way round is approaching. (5.NBT.A.3)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Stacking the Supply Cubes",short:"Volume",parts:[{std:["5.MD.C.3","5.MD.C.5"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Look at the stack. Answer out loud. Solve the last one on your paper.",desc:"understanding volume as the number of unit cubes that fill a solid, and using the volume formula",fig:()=>F.row([F.prism(4,3,2,{unit:34}),"A stack of supply cubes, 4 long, 3 deep and 2 high"]),qs:["How many unit cubes make up this stack? What makes this a question about volume and not about area?","Write the formula for the volume of a box like this, then use it on this stack.","A supply box measures 5 units by 4 units by 3 units. What is its volume? What is the total volume of two of those boxes side by side?"],key:["24 unit cubes. It is volume because it counts the space the solid fills, in cubic units, while area counts how much flat surface is covered, in square units. Partial: the count right with no account of why it is volume is approaching. (5.MD.C.3)","V = l × w × h, so V = 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 cubic units. V = b × h also works, where b is the 12 cubes in the base layer and h is the 2 layers. Partial: the formula right with no value worked out is approaching. (5.MD.C.5)","60 cubic units (5 × 4 × 3). Two of them together is 120 cubic units, because the volumes of the two boxes add. Partial: 60 found with no total for the two boxes is approaching. (5.MD.C.5)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Camp Grid",short:"Coordinates",parts:[{std:["5.G.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Look at the grid. Answer out loud.",desc:"naming points with ordered pairs and explaining how a coordinate system works",fig:()=>F.row([F.coordGrid({max:10,points:[[3,7,"A"],[6,2,"B"]],size:380}),"The camp map grid, marked in kilometers"]),qs:["Name the ordered pair for point A and the ordered pair for point B.","The two number lines are called axes. Where do they cross, what is that point called, and what are its coordinates?","In an ordered pair, what does the first number tell you, and what does the second number tell you?"],key:["A is (3, 7) and B is (6, 2). Partial: one of the two pairs right is approaching, and a reversed pair does not count. (5.G.A.1)","They cross at the bottom left corner, at the 0 on each line. That point is the origin, and its coordinates are (0, 0). Partial: two of the three parts answered is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","The first number tells how far to travel from the origin along the x-axis, the line that runs across. The second tells how far to travel along the y-axis, the line that runs up. The first number is the x-coordinate and the second is the y-coordinate. Partial: one of the two numbers explained is approaching. (5.G.A.1)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Frost Out of Nowhere",short:"Tiny Bits",parts:[{std:["5-PS1-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"using a model of particles too small to be seen to explain what happens to matter",html:()=>`<p class="big">Nobody spilled anything. Yet every morning the inside of the tent window is furred with frost. The ropes outside carry a white coat too.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("tent",80)}${F.icon("snowflake",80)}${F.icon("thermometer",80)}</div>`,qs:["No liquid water was poured on the window. Where did the frost come from?","Use particles too small to see to explain how water gets from a breath into the frost on a window.","A sealed sample bag of air brought from the tent weighs 2 g more than the same bag emptied. What does that prove about air?"],key:["From water vapor in the air inside the tent, which is water as particles too small to see. On the cold glass the particles slow down, stick, and build up as ice. (5-PS1-1)","Breath carries warm water vapor, which is enormous numbers of particles spread out through the air. They move about and some land on the cold window, where they lock together as solid ice. (5-PS1-1)","Air is matter. It is made of particles that have mass, so a bag with air in it weighs more than the same bag without it, even though you can see nothing in either. (5-PS1-1)"]},{std:["5-PS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"identifying materials from measured properties rather than from appearance",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four clear panels were found in the depot crate, all the same size and all looking the same.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Panel</th><th>Mass</th><th>Scratched by a steel nail?</th><th>Bends before it breaks?</th><th>Still solid at plus 5 degrees?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>N</td><td>250 g</td><td>yes</td><td>no, shatters</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>P</td><td>620 g</td><td>no</td><td>no, shatters</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Q</td><td>290 g</td><td>yes</td><td>yes, a little</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>R</td><td>300 g</td><td>yes</td><td>yes, a lot</td><td>yes</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The four materials are window glass, a slab of ice, acrylic sheet, and tough polycarbonate sheet.</p>`,qs:["Which panel is the ice? Name the result that settles it on its own.","Which panel is the glass, and name two results that point to it.","Panels Q and R are both light, scratchable, and bendy. Which is the polycarbonate, and which column decided it?"],key:["N. It is the only one that is not still solid at plus 5 degrees, which no other material on the list would do. (5-PS1-3)","P. It is by far the heaviest for its size and it is the only one a steel nail will not scratch, and it shatters rather than bending. (5-PS1-3)","R is the polycarbonate. The bending column decided it: R bends a lot before breaking while Q bends only a little. (5-PS1-3)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Sealed at the Depot",short:"Weigh In",parts:[{std:["5-PS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"using measurements to show that total weight is conserved through heating, cooling, and mixing",html:()=>`<p class="big">The expedition weighed three sealed flasks, cycled them between the cold store and the stove for a week, and weighed them again.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Sealed flask</th><th>What happened inside</th><th>At the start</th><th>After a week</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>froze and thawed seven times</td><td>740 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>warmed until some water boiled, then cooled</td><td>695 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>a spoon of drink powder stirred in, then frozen solid</td><td>758 g</td><td>758 g</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What does flask 1 weigh after a week? Explain.","Flask 2 boiled and there are drops on the inside of the lid. What does it weigh now?","Across all three flasks, state the rule in your own words."],key:["740 g. Freezing and thawing rearrange the particles between solid and liquid without adding or removing any, and the flask is sealed. (5-PS1-2)","695 g. Some water became vapor and then condensed on the lid, but every particle is still inside the sealed flask. (5-PS1-2)","In a sealed container, the total weight of matter stays the same no matter whether you heat it, cool it, or mix things into it. (5-PS1-2)"]},{std:["5-PS1-4"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"deciding from evidence whether mixing two substances made a new substance",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three mixings tried in the depot laboratory.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Mixing</th><th>What was seen</th></tr>
     <tr><td>hydrogen peroxide and a spoon of dried yeast</td><td>foam rose out of the flask within seconds, the flask warmed, and a glowing splint flared brighter when held in the foam</td></tr>
     <tr><td>powdered milk and sugar, shaken dry</td><td>a pale even powder; stirred into water, the drink tasted of both and nothing else happened</td></tr>
     <tr><td>iron filings, salt water, and air in an open dish</td><td>after three days the filings were rust brown and crumbly, and a magnet picked up far less than before</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which two mixings made a new substance? Give the evidence for each.","Which one is only a mixture, and what is the evidence?","The magnet picked up less after three days. Explain why that is good evidence of a new substance rather than a mixture."],key:["The peroxide and yeast, because a gas came off fast enough to foam, the flask warmed, and the gas made a glowing splint flare, and the iron in salt water and air, because the filings turned a different color and texture that cannot be undone. (5-PS1-4)","The powdered milk and sugar. Both were still there, unchanged, and nothing happened beyond mixing. (5-PS1-4)","A magnet picks up iron. If less is picked up, less iron is present, which means the iron was used up in making something else. A mixture would still hold all its iron. (5-PS1-4)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Growing Without Soil",short:"Plant Food",parts:[{std:["5-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing from measurements that plants get their material for growth from air and water",html:()=>`<p class="big">The depot grows salad under lamps with no soil at all. The roots sit in water with a trace of dissolved minerals, and the whole system is weighed.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sprout",76)}${F.icon("herb",84)}${F.icon("lamp",76)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>At planting</th><th>After six weeks</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the plants, dried</td><td>2 g</td><td>310 g</td></tr>
     <tr><td>minerals added to the water in total</td><td>0 g</td><td>9 g</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The plants also took in water, and air circulated freely around the leaves.</p>`,qs:["The plants gained 308 g of dry material but only 9 g of minerals were ever added. Where did the rest come from?","There was no soil in this system at all. What does that show about the old idea that plants eat soil?","Why were the minerals needed at all, if they are such a small part of the weight?"],key:["From air and water. Carbon dioxide from the air and water around the roots are built into the plant's material using energy from the lamps. (5-LS1-1)","That soil is not where the material comes from. A plant can build hundreds of grams of itself with no soil present, so the bulk must come from air and water. (5-LS1-1)","Minerals such as nitrogen are needed in small amounts for the plant's parts to work properly, the way a tiny amount of vitamin matters in a diet. They are necessary without being the bulk of the material. (5-LS1-1)"]},{std:["5-PS3-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Look at the chain. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a model to trace the energy in an animal's food back to the sun",html:()=>`<p class="big">One path energy takes in polar seas.</p>
     <p class="huge" style="font-size:2.2rem;margin:10px 0;text-align:center">${F.icon("sun",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("herb",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("crab",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("penguin",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("seal",46)}</p>
     <p class="big">Tiny floating plants are eaten by krill. Krill are eaten by penguins. Penguins are eaten by leopard seals.</p>`,qs:["Trace the energy in a seal's meal all the way back to its source.","The tiny floating plants live in the top layer of the sea, not deep down. Explain why.","Through the polar winter there is no sunlight for months. How can this chain keep going?"],key:["From the penguin, which got it from krill, which got it from the floating plants, which captured energy from sunlight. (5-PS3-1)","They need sunlight to make their food, and sunlight only reaches the upper layer of water. Deeper down there is not enough light to live on. (5-PS3-1)","It runs on energy stored earlier. Animals carry stored food as fat, krill feed on what grew in the light season, and many animals move away or slow down until the light returns. The energy still came from sunlight, just not today's. (5-PS3-1)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Slow Decay",short:"Matter Cycle",parts:[{std:["5-LS2-1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Look at the model. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing with a model how matter moves among plants, animals, decomposers, and the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">On the tundra the ground is frozen most of the year and thaws only a hand's depth in summer.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>How</th></tr>
     <tr><td>air and thawed soil</td><td>mosses, lichens, low shrubs</td><td>taken in during the short summer</td></tr>
     <tr><td>mosses and shrubs</td><td>caribou, lemmings, geese</td><td>grazing in summer</td></tr>
     <tr><td>dead plants and animal waste</td><td>bacteria and fungi in the thin thawed layer</td><td>breakdown, but only for a few weeks a year</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bacteria and fungi</td><td>air and soil</td><td>minerals and gases released slowly</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("herb",70)}${F.icon("deer",70)}${F.icon("mushroom",70)}${F.icon("snowflake",70)}</div>`,qs:["Dead plant material piles up on the tundra and can sit for centuries. Which row of the model explains why?","If decomposers work for only a few weeks a year, what does that mean for how fast minerals get back to the plants?","A warmer summer thaws the soil twice as deep. Predict two effects on this model.","Is this still a cycle, even though it runs slowly? Explain."],key:["The third row. Breakdown only happens in the thin layer that thaws, for a few weeks a year, so decomposers get very little time to work. (5-LS2-1)","They come back very slowly, so the soil stays poor and plants grow slowly. The whole system runs at a fraction of the speed of a warm place. (5-LS2-1)","Decomposers get more time and depth to work, so stored dead material breaks down faster, releasing more carbon dioxide into the air and more minerals into the soil, which could let plants grow more. (5-LS2-1)","Yes. The same matter still goes from air and soil into plants, through animals, through decomposers, and back to air and soil. Slow is not the same as broken. (5-LS2-1)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Down on the Ice",short:"Down",parts:[{std:["5-PS2-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing that the gravitational force Earth exerts on objects is directed down",html:()=>`<p class="big">An ice core slips out of a mitten. Inside the tent, a lamp hangs from the ridge pole on its cord.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("mitten",80)}${F.icon("lantern",80)}${F.icon("skis",80)}</div>`,qs:["Which way does the dropped core go? Which way does the hanging lamp cord point?","The expedition is near the South Pole. Which way is down there. Is it the same direction in space as down at the camp back home?","A crew member says gravity is weaker at the pole because everything feels heavy and slow in the cold. Name the real reason things feel heavy and slow there."],key:["Both go straight down toward the ground, or toward the ice underfoot. (5-PS2-1)","Down there is still toward the center of Earth. In space that is a different direction from down at home, almost the opposite if home is far north, but both point at Earth's center. (5-PS2-1)","Heavy clothing, thick gloves, deep snow, and cold stiff muscles and materials. Earth's pull is almost exactly the same there as anywhere else on the surface. (5-PS2-1)"]},{std:["5-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the story. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing with an example how the geosphere, biosphere, hydrosphere, and atmosphere interact",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Earth system has four parts. The geosphere is rock and soil. The biosphere is living things. The hydrosphere is all the water. The atmosphere is air.</p>
     <p class="big">Autumn air cools the sea surface until a skin of ice forms. As the ice grows it pushes salt out into the water below, and that saltier, heavier water sinks. Ice also reflects sunlight back to the sky instead of letting the water absorb it, so the sea cools faster still. Seals keep holes open in the ice to breathe, and the floating plants below slow down in the dim light.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("wave",72)}${F.icon("snowflake",72)}${F.icon("seal",72)}${F.icon("sun",72)}</div>`,qs:["Name the two spheres interacting when cold air turns sea water into ice.","Ice reflects sunlight back to the sky and the sea cools faster. Explain that as an interaction between spheres, and say why it speeds itself up.","Name one interaction in the story that involves living things, and say which spheres it connects."],key:["The atmosphere and the hydrosphere. Cold air takes heat out of the water until it freezes. (5-ESS2-1)","It connects the hydrosphere, now ice, with the atmosphere and with sunlight. More ice means more sunlight reflected away, which cools the water further, which makes more ice. The effect feeds itself. (5-ESS2-1)","Either seals keeping breathing holes open, which is biosphere acting on the frozen hydrosphere and getting air from the atmosphere, or the floating plants slowing in dim light, which is biosphere affected by sunlight through the hydrosphere. (5-ESS2-1)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Polar Sky",short:"Sky",parts:[{std:["5-ESS1-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing that the sun looks brightest because of how near it is, not because it gives off the most light",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three stars, with the light each gives off and its distance.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Star</th><th>Light it gives off</th><th>Distance from Earth</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the sun</td><td>1 unit</td><td>8 light-minutes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Alpha Centauri A, the nearest bright star</td><td>about 1.5 units</td><td>4.4 light-years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Canopus</td><td>about 10,000 units</td><td>310 light-years</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sun",76)}${F.icon("star",76)}${F.icon("telescope",76)}</div>`,qs:["Alpha Centauri A gives off about one and a half times the light of the sun. Why is it only a point of light in the polar sky?","Canopus gives off about 10,000 times the sun's light. Explain why it still does not light up the ice.","A crew member says Alpha Centauri A and the sun are nearly twins. So they should look about the same. Correct that, using the chart."],key:["It is 4.4 light-years away, while the sun is 8 light-minutes away. Even the nearest bright star is so far off that almost none of its light reaches here. (5-ESS1-1)","It is 310 light-years away. Light spreads out as it travels, and that distance is so enormous that it cancels out even a ten thousandfold advantage in light given off. (5-ESS1-1)","They are similar stars, and they look nothing alike from here because the sun is about 270,000 times nearer. How bright a star looks depends on its distance as much as on the light it makes. (5-ESS1-1)"]},{std:["5-ESS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"finding patterns in graphed data about shadows, day length, and the night sky",html:()=>`<p class="big">Hours of daylight recorded at the depot, which lies far south, on the first of six months.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Month</th><th>Hours of daylight at the depot</th><th>Hours of daylight at home, far north</th></tr>
     <tr><td>September</td><td>12</td><td>12.5</td></tr>
     <tr><td>November</td><td>19</td><td>9.8</td></tr>
     <tr><td>December</td><td>24</td><td>9.2</td></tr>
     <tr><td>January</td><td>22</td><td>9.6</td></tr>
     <tr><td>March</td><td>12</td><td>12.1</td></tr>
     <tr><td>May</td><td>5</td><td>14.3</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe the pattern in the depot column across the six months.","In December the depot has 24 hours of daylight and home has 9.2. Explain how both can be true on the same day.","In December the depot's shadows swing all the way around through the day instead of disappearing at night. What is causing the shadows to move?"],key:["Daylight climbs from 12 hours in September to 24 in December, then falls back to 12 in March and down to 5 by May. It rises and falls once over the year, with the longest days in December. (5-ESS1-2)","Earth is tilted on its axis. In December the southern end leans toward the sun, so places far south get constant daylight while places far north get very little. The seasons are opposite in the two halves of the world. (5-ESS1-2)","Earth is spinning, so the sun appears to circle around the horizon rather than setting, and the shadow swings the opposite way all the way around. (5-ESS1-2)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Ice and Fresh Water",short:"Water",parts:[{std:["5-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the amounts and percentages of water and fresh water in Earth's reservoirs",html:()=>`<p class="big">Where Earth's fresh water is, in rough figures. Remember that all the fresh water together is only about 3% of the water on Earth.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Where</th><th>Share of the fresh water</th><th>Roughly how long the water stays there</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Antarctic and Greenland ice sheets and glaciers</td><td>about 69%</td><td>thousands of years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>groundwater</td><td>about 30%</td><td>years to thousands of years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>lakes and rivers</td><td>under 1%</td><td>days to years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>water vapor in the air</td><td>a tiny fraction</td><td>about nine days</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which reservoir holds most of the world's fresh water, and about what share?","Rivers hold under 1% of the fresh water yet almost every city depends on one. How can both be true?","Water vapor in the air is a tiny fraction and stays about nine days. Why is a reservoir that small and that fast still important?"],key:["The ice sheets and glaciers, about 69%, more than two thirds of all fresh water. (5-ESS2-2)","The amount in a river at any moment is small, but it is constantly refilled as rain and snowmelt flow through. Cities depend on the flow passing through rather than on a stored amount. (5-ESS2-2)","Because every drop of rain and snow passes through it. The store is small but the water moves through it fast, which is what delivers fresh water to the land in the first place. (5-ESS2-2)"]},{std:["5-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the two reports. Answer out loud.",desc:"combining information about ways communities use science to protect resources and the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two research stations tried to cut the harm they did to the place they were studying.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Station</th><th>What they did</th><th>Result after two seasons</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Depot East</td><td>put up three wind turbines to replace most of the diesel generator's work</td><td>fuel flown in down by 70%, and far fewer fuel drums to handle</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Depot West</td><td>sorted all waste and shipped every bit of it home instead of burying it</td><td>nothing left buried at the site, and the melt pools nearby tested clean</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name two separate benefits Depot East got from the turbines.","Depot West ships its waste thousands of miles home. Why is that worth doing instead of burying it?","Combine the two reports into a plan for a new station being built next season."],key:["Far less fuel has to be flown in, which cuts cost and the pollution of flying it, and there are far fewer fuel drums to store, move, and risk spilling. (5-ESS3-1)","Cold slows decay almost to a stop, so buried waste stays for centuries, and meltwater can carry substances out of it into the pools and the sea. Removing it is the only way to leave the site clean. (5-ESS3-1)","Build with wind power to cut the fuel flown in, and sort and ship out all waste rather than burying it. The plan has to use something from each report. (5-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Design a Depot Shelter",short:"Design It",parts:[{std:["3-5-ETS1-1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Listen, then answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria for success and constraints",read:"The depot stores its supply crates outside. The wind blows steadily from one direction and piles drifting snow over the crates, so the crew spends an hour digging them out every morning. The team wants something that keeps the drift off the crates. Two people must be able to put it up in under an hour, it has to stay standing in a strong wind, and it can only be built from the poles, panels, and cable already at the depot, because the next supply flight is not for a month.",qs:["Say the problem in one sentence.","Name three things the finished design must do to count as a success.","Name two limits the team has to work inside."],key:["Drifting snow buries the supply crates every night, so the depot needs a way to keep the drift off them. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any three criteria from the story: keeps drifting snow off the crates, can be put up by two people in under an hour, and stays standing in a strong wind. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any two constraints: only poles, panels, and cable already at the depot, and no new materials for a month. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing possible solutions against the criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three designs, each left out for a week of the same weather.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Snow on the crates after a week</th><th>Put-up time, two people</th><th>Stood up to the wind?</th><th>Depot materials only?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>S: a flat roof on four poles over the crates</td><td>knee deep, the roof filled and sagged</td><td>40 min</td><td>no, two poles bent</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>T: an upright fence of panels set 10 m upwind of the crates</td><td>ankle deep, with the big drift out at the fence</td><td>50 min</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>U: a bought dome tent over the whole stack</td><td>none</td><td>25 min</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which design kept the crates clearest? Explain why it is still not the answer here.","Design T leaves the drift out at the fence rather than stopping snow altogether. Explain why that still solves the problem.","Design S failed on two counts. Name both."],key:["U kept them completely clear, but it has to be bought, so it breaks the limit that only depot materials may be used. (3-5-ETS1-2)","The point was to stop the crew digging crates out. A fence upwind slows the wind so the snow drops before it reaches the crates, which leaves only ankle deep snow on them. Where the drift lands does not matter as long as it is not on the crates. (3-5-ETS1-2)","It let knee deep snow build on the crates, so it failed the main criterion, and two poles bent, so it failed to stand up to the wind. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the test plan. Answer out loud.",desc:"planning a fair test with controlled variables and naming the failure points to improve",html:()=>`<p class="big">The team tested how far upwind to set the fence.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Trial 1</th><th>Trial 2</th></tr>
     <tr><td>distance upwind of the crates</td><td>4 m</td><td>10 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>fence height</td><td>2 m</td><td>2 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>days of the same wind</td><td>5</td><td>5</td></tr>
     <tr><td>snow on the crates</td><td>40 cm</td><td>9 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>what went wrong</td><td>the drift grew past the fence and reached the crates</td><td>snow worked under the bottom edge of the panels</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What was held the same, and what was changed? Why does that make the test fair?","Suppose trial 2 had used a taller fence as well. Could you still say the distance caused the improvement? Explain.","Name one change that would fix the failure in each trial."],key:["The fence height and the number of days of the same wind were held the same, and only the distance upwind changed, so the difference in snow on the crates can be traced to the distance. (3-5-ETS1-3)","No. Two things would have changed, distance and height, so you could not tell which one did the work. That is not a fair test. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Trial 1 was too close, so move the fence further upwind or make it taller so the drift has room to form short of the crates. Trial 2 lost snow under the bottom edge, so seal or bank the base, or leave a deliberate gap at the bottom so wind sweeps the base clear. Any fix tied to the stated failure counts. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:31,title:"Trade Before 1492",short:"Old Trade",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.1"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing pre-Columbian settlements through the goods and routes that connected them",html:()=>`<p class="big">Nations across North America traded with each other over long distances, centuries before any European ship arrived. Archaeologists find goods hundreds of miles from where they were made.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Goods</th><th>Where they came from</th><th>Found as far away as</th></tr>
     <tr><td>shells</td><td>the Gulf and Pacific coasts</td><td>villages deep inland in the Southwest and the woodlands</td></tr>
     <tr><td>obsidian, a volcanic glass that holds a sharp edge</td><td>mountain areas in the West</td><td>sites hundreds of miles away in every direction</td></tr>
     <tr><td>copper</td><td>around the Great Lakes</td><td>the Southeast and the Great Plains</td></tr>
     <tr><td>turquoise</td><td>the desert Southwest</td><td>as far south as central Mexico</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("shell",70)}${F.icon("gem",70)}${F.icon("coin",70)}${F.icon("map",70)}</div>`,qs:["Shells from the coast turn up in villages deep inland. What does that tell you about how people lived before 1492?","Obsidian holds a sharp edge. Why would a nation with no volcano nearby want it badly enough to trade for it?","There were no horses or wheeled carts in North America then. How could goods travel hundreds of miles anyway?","Name one thing archaeologists can work out from a traded object, and one thing they cannot."],key:["That nations were connected over long distances by trade, knew about one another, and had routes, agreements, and people who traveled. They were not isolated villages. Partial: saying they traded with nothing said about routes, agreements or distance is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","It makes the sharpest tools available, for knives, scrapers, and points. A better cutting edge made every daily job easier, so it was worth trading valuable goods for. Partial: saying it is sharp with no account of why that was worth trading for is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","On foot and by canoe along rivers and coasts, and usually hand to hand: each nation traded with its neighbors, and a shell could change hands many times before it ended up far inland. Partial: naming walking or canoes with nothing said about goods changing hands is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","They can work out that the two places were connected, roughly when, and sometimes along which route. They cannot tell from the object alone who carried it, whether it was traded, given, or taken, or what it meant to the people who held it. Partial: one of the can and the cannot given is approaching. (HSS-5.1)"]}]},{id:32,title:"Spanish Routes Overland",short:"Overland",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.2"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing early Spanish explorations across the Americas and what the expeditions reported",html:()=>`<p class="big">Spain sent expeditions overland from its bases in Mexico and the Caribbean. These were long marches, not voyages.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Expedition</th><th>Route</th><th>What they reported</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Cabeza de Vaca, 1528 to 1536</td><td>shipwrecked in Florida, then walked west across the Gulf coast and Texas to Mexico</td><td>an account of many different nations, their farming, and their towns</td></tr>
     <tr><td>de Soto, 1539 to 1542</td><td>inland across the Southeast as far as the Mississippi River</td><td>large towns along the rivers, and no gold</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Coronado, 1540 to 1542</td><td>north from Mexico through the Southwest to the Great Plains</td><td>Pueblo towns of stone and adobe, grasslands and buffalo, and no cities of gold</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("compass",76)}${F.icon("cactus",76)}${F.icon("horse",76)}</div>`,qs:["All three expeditions were looking for something they did not find. What was it?","Cabeza de Vaca's journey took eight years and covered thousands of miles on foot. Why would an account like his be valuable even without gold?","Coronado reported grasslands and buffalo rather than cities of gold. How would a report like that change what Spain did next?","These were overland routes. Name one difficulty an overland expedition faced that a sea voyage did not."],key:["Cities of gold, or great riches. All three reported that they found none. Partial: naming gold with no answer about whether they found it is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","It was the first detailed European description of the nations, lands, farming, and towns across that whole stretch of the continent, which no map or earlier account had. Information was valuable in itself. Partial: saying it was interesting with nothing said about information being valuable in itself is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","It made the north look less worth the expense of conquest, so Spain concentrated on the areas it already held and on Mexico and the Caribbean rather than pouring expeditions into the Plains. Partial: saying Spain was disappointed with no account of what it did next is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","Any real difficulty: food and water had to be found along the way rather than carried in a hold, the party depended on the nations whose land they crossed, there were no charts of the interior, and there was no quick way home. Partial: naming a hardship that a sea voyage shared, such as bad weather, is approaching. (HSS-5.2)"]},{std:["HSS-5.3"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the two accounts of the same agreement. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing how two sides understood the same land agreement",html:()=>`<p class="big">In 1737 a colony and the Lenape nation disagreed about a land deal. Here is how each side described it.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>The colony's account</th><th>The Lenape account</th></tr>
     <tr><td>an old paper allowed land as far as a man could walk in a day and a half</td><td>the understanding had been an ordinary day and a half's walk, at a walking pace, along the river</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the colony hired three of the fastest runners and cleared a path for them in advance</td><td>runners sprinting on a cleared path was not what either side had meant by walking</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the walk covered about 65 miles, and the colony claimed all of it</td><td>the claim took far more land than had ever been agreed</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name the single word the two sides understood differently, and explain each meaning.","The colony cleared a path in advance. Why does that detail matter to the dispute?","Both accounts could be describing the same events honestly. Explain how two honest accounts can disagree this much.","What does this case show about why written agreements between the two sides often ended in conflict?"],key:["Walk. The Lenape understood an ordinary day and a half of walking at a normal pace. The colony treated it as however far the fastest possible man could travel in that time. Partial: the word named with one of the two meanings given is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","It shows the colony was planning to stretch the agreement as far as it could go rather than carrying it out as understood. Clearing a path is not what happens on an ordinary walk. Partial: noting the cleared path with nothing said about what it shows is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","Each is telling what it believed had been agreed. The written paper was vague, the two sides had different customs for land agreements, and translation between languages left room for very different readings. Partial: saying one side lied is not the answer the question asks for; naming the vague wording with nothing about custom or translation is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","That the wording, the language it was written in, and who got to interpret it all mattered enormously, and one side usually controlled all three. Agreements that were vague could be stretched by whoever held the power. Partial: saying agreements were broken with nothing said about who controlled the wording is approaching. (HSS-5.3)"]}]},{id:33,title:"Belief, Dissent and Causes",short:"Colonies",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.4"],n:4,dur:120,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the religious institutions of the colonial era and the limits of colonial toleration",html:()=>`<p class="big">Several colonies were founded by groups who had been punished at home for their beliefs. What they then did about other people's beliefs varied a great deal.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Colony</th><th>Founded by or for</th><th>How it treated other beliefs</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Massachusetts Bay</td><td>Puritans seeking to build a community run on their own beliefs</td><td>required agreement with the colony's beliefs; those who taught otherwise were put on trial or expelled</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Rhode Island</td><td>Roger Williams, expelled from Massachusetts</td><td>separated belief from government and admitted people of any belief</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Maryland</td><td>a refuge for English Catholics</td><td>passed an early toleration act, which was later repealed</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Pennsylvania</td><td>William Penn and the Quakers</td><td>wide toleration, and treated land agreements with the Lenape more carefully than its neighbors</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Massachusetts Bay was founded by people who had been punished for their beliefs, and then punished others. How can both be true?","What did Roger Williams do differently in Rhode Island, and why was that unusual at the time?","Maryland's toleration act was later repealed. What does that tell you about how secure a right is when it rests on one law?","Pick two colonies on the chart and name the clearest difference between them."],key:["They wanted freedom to live by their own beliefs, not freedom for everyone to believe differently. Having built a community around one set of beliefs, they treated disagreement as a threat to it. Partial: naming the contradiction with no account of how both can be true is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","He kept belief and government separate and admitted people of any belief, which almost no government anywhere did then. Most places assumed a state had to enforce one set of beliefs to hold together. Partial: saying he allowed other beliefs with nothing said about why that was unusual is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","That a right created by an ordinary law can be removed by an ordinary law. It is one reason the Bill of Rights was later written into the Constitution rather than left to ordinary acts. Partial: saying the law was repealed with nothing said about how secure such a right is is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","Any supported pair. For example Massachusetts Bay expelled people who taught other beliefs while Rhode Island admitted anyone, even though both were founded by people fleeing punishment for belief. Partial: two colonies named with no difference between them is approaching. (HSS-5.4)"]},{std:["HSS-5.5"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"sorting the causes of the American Revolution into long-term and immediate causes",html:()=>`<p class="big">Historians sort causes by how long they had been building.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Long-term or immediate?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>colonies had run their own assemblies for well over a century</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>an ocean crossing took six to eight weeks, so London could not supervise closely</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Parliament closed the port of Boston in 1774</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>ideas about natural rights spread through pamphlets in the 1760s and 1770s</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>soldiers and colonists came into open fighting at Lexington in April 1775</td><td>?</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Sort the five causes into long-term and immediate.","Pick one long-term cause and explain how it made the immediate ones more likely to lead to war.","Could the immediate causes have happened without the long-term ones? Explain your thinking.","Why is it useful for a historian to separate the two kinds of cause?"],key:["Long-term: self-governing assemblies for over a century, the distance and slow communication, and the spread of ideas about natural rights, which sits between the two and is reasonable either way with an explanation. Immediate: closing the port of Boston in 1774 and the fighting at Lexington in 1775. Partial: three or four of the five sorted right is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","Any supported answer. For example a century of running their own assemblies meant colonists already saw themselves as governing their own affairs, so closing a port and stripping an assembly looked like an attack on something they had, not a new rule. Partial: a long-term cause named with no link to the immediate ones is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","Any reasoned answer. A port closure in a place with no habit of self-government and no spread of rights ideas might have been met with grumbling rather than a united rebellion. The long-term causes decided how people read the immediate ones. Partial: a yes or no with no thinking behind it is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","It keeps a historian from mistaking the spark for the whole explanation. The immediate cause tells you when something happened; the long-term causes tell you why it was possible at all. Partial: saying it is tidier with no account of when against why is approaching. (HSS-5.5)"]},{std:["HSS-5.6"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing who took part in the Revolution and what each group hoped for",html:()=>`<p class="big">The war was not fought only by soldiers in uniform.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Who</th><th>What they did</th></tr>
     <tr><td>African American soldiers</td><td>thousands served on the American side, including the First Rhode Island Regiment; many had been promised freedom for serving, a promise kept unevenly</td></tr>
     <tr><td>African Americans who joined the British</td><td>Britain offered freedom to those who left owners who were in rebellion, and thousands took it; many left with the British fleet at the end</td></tr>
     <tr><td>women</td><td>ran farms and shops while men were away, made cloth and cartridges, carried messages, nursed and cooked in camps, and a few served in disguise</td></tr>
     <tr><td>American Indian nations</td><td>some allied with Britain, some with the Americans, some tried to stay out; most were trying to protect their land whichever way they turned</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Both sides recruited African Americans by offering freedom. Explain why that offer was powerful on either side.","Name two things women did that the war could not have gone on without.","Most American Indian nations were weighing the same question whichever side they chose. What was it?","Pick one group on the chart and say what they hoped for and whether the end of the war delivered it."],key:["Because freedom was the thing enslaved people wanted most, and neither side was offering it as a matter of principle, so a person had to judge which promise was more likely to be kept. Partial: naming freedom with nothing said about judging which promise would be kept is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Any two: running the farms and shops that fed and supplied everyone, making cloth when imports were boycotted, making cartridges, carrying messages, and nursing and cooking for the army. Partial: one thing named is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Which side was less likely to take their land. Independence or British rule mattered to them mainly through its effect on settlement pressure. Partial: saying they wanted to be left alone with no mention of land is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Any supported answer. For example many African American soldiers hoped for freedom and the promise was kept unevenly, with slavery continuing in much of the new country; American Indian nations hoped to hold their land and instead faced far greater settlement pressure after 1783. Partial: a hope named with no answer about whether the war delivered it is approaching. (HSS-5.6)"]}]},{id:34,title:"Amending and Moving West",short:"New Nation",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.7"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing how the Constitution can be amended and what that has been used for",html:()=>`<p class="big">The writers knew they could not foresee everything, so they built in a way to change the document.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Step</th><th>What it takes</th></tr>
     <tr><td>propose</td><td>a two thirds vote in both houses of Congress</td></tr>
     <tr><td>ratify</td><td>agreement by three quarters of the states</td></tr>
     </table>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Amendment</th><th>What it did</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1st to 10th, 1791</td><td>the Bill of Rights, listing rights the government may not take away</td></tr>
     <tr><td>13th, 1865</td><td>ended slavery in the United States</td></tr>
     <tr><td>19th, 1920</td><td>said the vote could not be denied on account of sex</td></tr>
     <tr><td>26th, 1971</td><td>set the voting age at 18</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name the two steps an amendment has to pass, and the fraction needed at each.","Only 27 amendments have passed in more than two centuries. What does the chart suggest about why so few?","Why would the writers deliberately make changing the Constitution difficult rather than easy?","Pick one amendment on the chart and explain what it changed about who counted as a full member of the country."],key:["Proposed by a two thirds vote in both houses of Congress, then ratified by three quarters of the states. Partial: one of the two steps with its fraction is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","The thresholds are very high. A two thirds vote in both houses and then three quarters of the states means an amendment needs agreement far beyond a simple majority, so only widely supported changes get through. Partial: saying it is hard with no fraction named is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","So that a temporary majority or a passing mood could not rewrite the basic rules. Difficulty protects rights from being stripped away quickly, though it also slows down needed change. Partial: saying it protects the Constitution with no account of a passing majority is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","Any supported answer. The 13th ended slavery, so millions of people were no longer held as property; the 19th meant women could not be denied the vote because they were women; the 26th brought in citizens aged 18 to 20. Partial: an amendment named with nothing said about who it brought in is approaching. (HSS-5.7)"]},{std:["HSS-5.8"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining how physical geography shaped where people settled and how they traveled",html:()=>`<p class="big">Geography decided a great deal about where towns grew and which routes people took.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Effect on settlement and travel</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the Appalachian Mountains</td><td>a long barrier; travel went through a few gaps such as the Cumberland Gap</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the fall line, where rivers drop from upland to coastal plain</td><td>boats could come up no further, and the falls turned mills, so cities grew at those points</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the Mississippi and Ohio river system</td><td>a highway into the interior, so farms spread along navigable water first</td></tr>
     <tr><td>South Pass through the Rocky Mountains</td><td>a broad, gentle crossing that wagons could manage, so overland trails funnelled through it</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Why did cities grow at the fall line rather than a few miles upstream or downstream?","Travel through the Appalachians went through a few gaps. What does that do to the places that sit in those gaps?","Why would farms spread along navigable rivers before they spread across open country with no river?","South Pass is gentle enough for wagons. Explain how one feature like that can decide the route of an entire migration."],key:["Boats could travel no further upstream, so goods had to be unloaded there, and the falls themselves provided power for mills. A place where cargo changes hands and power is available becomes a city. Partial: one of the unloading and the mill power named is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","They become busy and valuable, because everyone traveling that way has to pass through. Towns, forts, trading posts, and tolls appear at a gap. Partial: saying people go through them with nothing said about what grows there is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","A farmer needs to get a crop to market. On a navigable river a crop can be shipped cheaply, so the land is worth farming. Land with no cheap way out is worth far less, however good the soil. Partial: naming water for the farm rather than a way to market is approaching, since the question is about selling the crop. (HSS-5.8)","Wagons could not cross steep peaks at all, so a single crossing that wagons could manage became the only practical route. Thousands of people then funnelled through it, and trails, supply posts, and later rail routes followed the same line. Partial: naming the pass with nothing said about wagons being unable to cross elsewhere is approaching. (HSS-5.8)"]},{std:["HSS-5.9"],n:5,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. A map is fine to look at.",desc:"locating mountain and desert western states and naming their capitals",html:()=>`<div class="figure">${F.icon("mountain",96)}${F.icon("map",96)}</div>
     <p class="big">Questions about the interior West.</p>`,qs:["Name four states the Rocky Mountains run through.","What is the capital of Colorado, and what is the capital of Nevada?","Which state has the Grand Canyon, and what is its capital?","Four states meet at a single point called Four Corners. Name them.","How many states are there in all, and name two western states that have no coastline."],key:["Any four of Montana, Idaho, Wyoming, Colorado, Utah, and New Mexico. Partial: two or three of the four named is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Denver, Colorado and Carson City, Nevada. Note if the student names Las Vegas or Reno, which is the usual slip. Partial: one of the two capitals right is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Arizona, capital Phoenix. (HSS-5.9)","Utah, Colorado, Arizona, and New Mexico. Partial: three of the four named is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","50 states. Any two landlocked western states: Nevada, Utah, Arizona, Colorado, New Mexico, Wyoming, Montana, Idaho. Partial: the count right with one landlocked state named is approaching. (HSS-5.9)"]}]}];const WC=FLUENCY.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;const readPart=STOPS[0].parts[0];readPart.n=WC;readPart.key[0]=readPart.key[0].replace("__WC__",WC);readPart.desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level passage read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={3:{id:"L3",title:"Level Up: Cite the Evidence",std:["RL.6.1"],needs:["RL.5.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use the story and cite your evidence.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>What kind of leader is Nadia’s aunt? Cite two details from the story that support your answer.</li><li>The story never says that Nadia is a careful worker. What evidence in the story supports that idea?</li></ol>${F.passage(SLED,SLED_TITLE,true)}`,key:["Careful and clear, and fair when it counts. Any two details: she had said the trail to the depot was no place to learn, she gives exact rules (“Stay in my tracks. If you cannot see my sled, stop and wait for me”), and she hands over the spare team when the storm leaves her short of drivers. Two cited details are needed. (RL.6.1, cites evidence to support an analysis)","“She checked every buckle twice, the way she had watched her aunt do it.” Checking twice, and copying how her aunt does it, is evidence of care. (RL.6.1, inference with cited evidence)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Evidence on the Ice",std:["RI.6.1"],needs:["RI.5.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Cite evidence from the article.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The article never uses the word safety. Cite two details that show measuring the ice is a safety job.</li><li>Does the drill do more than keep the team safe? Cite the evidence for your answer.</li></ol>${F.passage(ICE,ICE_TITLE,true)}`,key:["Any two: the rule that a loaded sled needs at least twenty centimeters of solid ice under it, the warning that dark patches are often thin ice with seawater showing through, and that someone drills and measures before a sled crosses. Two cited details are needed. (RI.6.1, cites evidence to support an analysis)","Yes. The core pulled out of the ice has bands, one for each winter, and counting them tells the scientists how many winters that ice has lasted. That is research, not safety. (RI.6.1, cites evidence)"]},7:{id:"L7",title:"Level Up: Divide the Fractions",std:["6.NS.A.1"],needs:["5.NF.B.3"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper. Then tell your teacher your answers.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>3 people share 1/2 of a kilogram of trail mix equally. How much does each person get?</li><li>How many 3/4 liter bottles can be filled from 6 liters of stove fuel?</li><li>2/3 ÷ 1/4 = ?</li></ol>`,key:["1/6 of a kilogram. 1/2 ÷ 3, and half of a kilogram split 3 ways is 1/6 each. (6.NS.A.1)","8 bottles. 6 ÷ 3/4 = 6 × 4/3 = 24/3 = 8. (6.NS.A.1)","8/3, which is 2 2/3. 2/3 ÷ 1/4 = 2/3 × 4/1. (6.NS.A.1)"]},9:{id:"L9",title:"Level Up: Word Choice",std:["RL.6.4"],needs:["RL.5.4"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use the story.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The author wrote that the northern lights “were awake.” Why is awake a stronger choice than were shining? What does it make you feel about the lights?</li><li>The last line says the camp “slowly remembered how to talk.” What does that word choice tell you about how the people felt?</li></ol>${F.passage(LIGHTS,LIGHTS_TITLE,true)}`,key:["Awake treats the lights as something alive that moves on its own, so they feel surprising and a little powerful. Shining would only describe light and would give the sky no personality. (RL.6.4, impact of word choice on meaning and tone)","They were so amazed that they had gone completely silent, and it took a moment to come back to normal. It gives the ending a hushed, awed tone. (RL.6.4, impact of word choice on tone)"]},11:{id:"L11",title:"Level Up: Pronoun Patrol",std:["L.6.1"],needs:["L.5.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each sentence out loud the right way.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Fix it: “If a driver loses the trail, you should stop and wait.”</li><li>Fix it: “Him and me checked the fuel cans.”</li><li>Add one word to show that nobody helped: “We dug out the buried tent ____.”</li></ol>`,key:["If a driver loses the trail, he or she should stop and wait. Also correct: If drivers lose the trail, they should stop and wait. (L.6.1, fixing a shift in pronoun person)","He and I checked the fuel cans. (L.6.1, pronouns in the subjective case)","We dug out the buried tent ourselves. (L.6.1, intensive pronoun)"]}};const PASSAGE_STD=["RL.5.1","RL.5.4","RI.5.1","W.5.8","RL.6.1","RI.6.1","RL.6.4"];const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function strips(){const dens=[1,2,3,4,5,6,8,12],W=600,h=36;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W+8} ${dens.length*(h+6)+4}" width="100%" style="max-width:640px" role="img" aria-label="Fraction strips for halves, thirds, fourths, fifths, sixths, eighths, and twelfths">`;dens.forEach((d,r)=>{for(let k=0;k<d;k++){const x=4+k*W/d,y=2+r*(h+6);s+=`<rect x="${x}" y="${y}" width="${W/d}" height="${h}" fill="${r%2?"var(--sea-soft)":"var(--surface)"}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="${x+W/d/2}" y="${y+h/2}" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" font-size="${d>8?14:17}" fill="var(--ink)">${d===1?"1":"1/"+d}</text>`}});return s+"</svg>"}const frames=list=>`<ul class="starters">${list.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;function sentHtml(st){const has=c=>st&&st.std&&st.std.includes(c);if(has("W.5.2"))return frames(["A polar research team lives ____.","One thing to know is ____.","Another fact is ____.","For example, ____.","Now you know ____."]);if(has("W.5.8"))return frames(["Note: ____ (Source ____)","My sources: 1. ____ by ____. 2. ____ by ____.","Whales travel to polar water in summer because ____ and ____."]);if(has("SL.5.4"))return frames(["This report tells how ____.","First, ____. Next, ____.","After that, ____.","Last, ____.","That is how ____."]);if(has("W.5.1"))return frames(["I think the team should ____.","One reason is ____, because ____.","In addition, ____.","For instance, ____.","That is why ____."]);if(has("W.5.3"))return frames(["At first, ____.","“____,” said ____.","By then, ____.","Once ____, she ____.","Finally, ____."]);if(has("SL.5.2"))return frames(["The report is mostly about ____.","It explains that ____ and ____.","In short, ____."]);if(has("RI.5.9"))return frames(["Source 1 says ____.","Source 2 adds that ____.","Putting them together, ____."]);if(has("RI.5.1")||has("RI.6.1"))return frames(["The article says, “____.”","I know ____ because the article says, “____.”"]);return frames(["The story says, “____.”","I know ____ because the story says, “____.”","The words “____” show that ____."])}const TOOLS={chunk:{name:"Prefix, suffix, and root chart",std:["RF.5.3"],html:()=>F.table(["Word part","What it means"],[["ir-, in-, un-, non-","not or the opposite of"],["mis-","wrongly"],["sub-","under"],["trans-","across"],["re-","again or back"],["-tion","the act or result of"],["-able, -ible","can be"],["-ist","a person who does"],["geo","earth"],["marine","of the sea"],["port","carry"]])+`<p class="small">Cover the prefix and the suffix, read the middle, then put the word back together.</p>`},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","5.OA.A.1","5.MD.C.5","6.NS.A.1"],html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const row=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===row&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[row,k];r()})},strips:{name:"Fraction strips",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4"],html:strips},frac:{name:"Adding fractions steps",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2"],html:()=>`<ol class="starters"><li>Find a common denominator. List multiples: 4: 4, 8, <b>12</b> &nbsp; 6: 6, <b>12</b></li><li>Make equivalent fractions. Multiply the top and the bottom by the same number: 3/4 = 9/12, 1/6 = 2/12.</li><li>Add or subtract the numerators. Keep the denominator.</li><li>Mixed numbers: add or subtract the wholes, then the fractions. Regroup one whole as a fraction if you need to.</li></ol><p class="small">Check it: is the answer near 0, 1/2, or 1? Does that make sense?</p>`},fdiv:{name:"Fraction formula card",std:["5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],html:()=>`<ul class="starters"><li>A fraction is a division problem: a/b = a ÷ b.</li><li>Improper to mixed: 23/6 means 23 ÷ 6, which is 3 with 5 left over, so 3 5/6.</li><li>Fraction times fraction: multiply the tops, then multiply the bottoms.</li><li>A whole number can be written over 1: 7 = 7/1.</li><li>“Of” means multiply: 5/6 of 24 is 5/6 × 24.</li></ul>`},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:["W.5.1","W.5.2","W.5.3","W.5.8","SL.5.2","SL.5.4","RI.5.9","RL.5.1","RI.5.1","RL.6.1","RI.6.1"],html:sentHtml},figs:{name:"Simile and metaphor card",std:["RL.5.4","RL.6.4"],html:()=>`<ul class="starters"><li>Simile: compares two things with like or as. “The tent snapped like a flag.”</li><li>Metaphor: says one thing is another thing. “The trail was a white ribbon.”</li><li>Ask: what two things are compared? How are they alike? How does the word make you feel?</li></ul>`},tense:{name:"Verb tense card",std:["L.5.1","L.6.1"],html:()=>F.table(["Tense","How to build it","Example"],[["Past perfect","had + verb","The storm had already passed."],["Present perfect","has or have + verb","The wind has blown all week."],["Future perfect","will have + verb","By Friday we will have arrived."],["Tense shift","keep one time in a sentence","We packed and loaded, not packed and loads."],["Paired words","not only / but also, both / and, either / or, neither / nor","Not only was it cold, but it was also windy."]])},info:{name:"Informative organizer and word bank",std:["W.5.2"],html:()=>F.table(["Part","What to write"],[["Title","Name your topic"],["Opening","What is this page about?"],["Facts","Fact 1, Fact 2, Fact 3 (group the ones that go together)"],["Closing","Sum it up in one sentence"]])+`<p class="small">Linking words: also, another, for example, because, in contrast, as a result. Topic words: base camp, sled, rations, depot, stove, below zero.</p>`}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"chunk",std:["RF.5.3"],msg:"The chart gives the meaning of the prefixes, suffixes, and roots, so count the word reading as supported, not independent."},{t:"mult",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the products and the common multiples. Rate on whether the student sets up the problem and explains it."},{t:"strips",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4"],msg:"With fraction strips the student can see the size of each part or share. Rate on whether the student can write the equation and explain it."},{t:"frac",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2"],msg:"The steps chart walks through finding a common denominator and regrouping. Count these answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"fdiv",std:["5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],msg:"The formula card gives the rules for fractions as division and for multiplying fractions. Count this stop as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.5.2"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.5.8"],msg:"The frames show how to label a note and list a source, so count the list of sources as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.5.4"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the order words and the closing, so count those checks as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["RL.5.1","RI.5.1","RL.6.1","RI.6.1"],msg:"The frames remind the student to quote. Rate on the accuracy of the quote."},{t:"figs",std:["RL.5.4","RL.6.4"],msg:"The card tells what a simile and a metaphor are, so count the simile and metaphor questions as supported."},{t:"tense",std:["L.5.1","L.6.1"],msg:"The verb tense card shows how to build each tense and lists the paired words. Count those items as supported."},{t:"info",std:["W.5.2"],msg:"The organizer and word bank supply the structure, the linking words, and the topic words. Count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"passage",std:["RL.5.1","RL.5.4","RI.5.1","RL.6.1","RI.6.1","RL.6.4"],msg:"If the passage was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"passage",std:["W.5.8"],msg:"If the sources were read to the student, the notes still count for W.5.8, but write down that the sources were read aloud."},{t:"mult",std:["5.OA.A.1","5.MD.C.5"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the products, so rate the grouping symbols and the volume formula on the reasoning, not on the arithmetic."},{t:"sent",std:["W.5.1","W.5.3"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, the time words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.5.2"],msg:"The frames give the shape of a summary, so count the summary as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["RI.5.9"],msg:"The frames prompt the student to name each source and join them, so rate on the facts chosen rather than on the joining words."},{t:"passage",std:["RI.5.9"],msg:"If the two sources were read to the student, this stop shows listening, not reading. The integration of the two still counts."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
